    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6, given that \\( n \\) divided by 6 has a remainder of 1.\n\nHere are the steps to solve the problem:\n\n1. **Express \\( n \\) in terms of its remainder when divided by 6:**\n   Since \\( n \\) leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 6, we can write:\n   \\[\n   n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n   This means that \\( n \\) can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   n = 6k + 1\n   \\]\n   for some integer \\( k \\).\n\n2. **Calculate \\( n + 2010 \\):**\n   Substitute \\( n = 6k + 1 \\) into \\( n + 2010 \\):\n   \\[\n   n + 2010 = (6k + 1) + 2010\n   \\]\n   Simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   n + 2010 = 6k + 2011\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6:**\n   We need to find \\( 6k + 2011 \\pmod{6} \\). Notice that \\( 2011 \\) can be written in terms of a multiple of 6 plus a remainder when divided by 6:\n   \\[\n   2011 \\div 6 = 335 \\text{ remainder } 1\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   2011 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n   Substituting this back into our expression:\n   \\[\n   n + 2010 \\equiv 6k + 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the remainder when \\( n + 2010 \\) is divided by 6 is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).",
